+# Product Requirements Document (PRD)
+
+**Project**: Pulseboard  
+**Version**: 1.0.0  
+**Date**: 2026-08-10  
+**Status**: Planning  
+**Scale**: Level 2
+
+## Information Sources
+
+This plan uses only `project.md`, `product-contract.md`, `evidence-contract.md`, and `context.md`. No research, architecture, UX strategy, design system, constitution, domain model, or brownfield artifact was supplied. The planning choices below are reversible assumptions, not claims that those artifacts exist.
+
+## Executive Summary
+
+Pulseboard helps meeting facilitators turn uploaded transcripts into reviewed action items. It requires a facilitator to confirm text, owner, and due date before one reminder can be sent. The four-week pilot targets five teams, 80% proposal review, and 30% fewer unowned overdue actions.
+
+## Product Vision & Strategy
+
+### Vision Statement
+
+Make meeting follow-through calm and accountable: turn transcripts into clear, owned actions without surrendering control to automation.
+
+### Strategic Objectives
+
+1. Extract reviewable action proposals from existing transcripts.
+2. Make human confirmation a genuine gate before any outbound reminder.
+3. Prove pilot value through proposal review and ownership metrics.
+
+### Success Definition
+
+- Five pilot teams use the product within four weeks.
+- At least 80% of proposals are reviewed.
+- Unowned overdue actions decline by 30% from each team's pilot baseline.
+- A real request-role probe cannot read another workspace's transcripts or actions.
+
+## Market Analysis
+
+### Target Market
+
+Meeting facilitators on pilot teams. Market size, competition, and growth are not asserted because no research was supplied.
+
+### Market Opportunity
+
+The pilot addresses teams that already have transcripts but lack a controlled path to owned follow-through.
+
+## User Analysis
+
+### User Personas
+
+**Meeting facilitator**: needs to turn a transcript into accurate, reviewed, owned actions and avoid accidental automation.
+
+**Action owner**: needs one clear due-dated responsibility and an observable reminder outcome.
+
+### User Journey Map: Transcript to Follow-through
+
+1. Facilitator uploads an existing transcript.
+2. Pulseboard presents workspace-scoped action proposals.
+3. Facilitator edits and reviews text, owner, and due date.
+4. Facilitator explicitly confirms the action.
+5. The configured provider makes one reminder attempt and Pulseboard shows status.
+
+## Functional Requirements
+
+### Core Features
+
+#### Secure workspace and transcript proposals
+
+- **Description**: Upload a transcript and extract proposed actions in its workspace.
+- **Acceptance Criteria**: Proposals are reviewable; a real cross-workspace request cannot read the transcript or its actions.
+- **Priority**: P0.
+
+#### Human review and confirmation
+
+- **Description**: A facilitator edits and explicitly confirms action text, owner, and due date.
+- **Acceptance Criteria**: Scheduling is impossible until all three fields are confirmed; proposal and confirmed states are distinguishable.
+- **Priority**: P0.
+
+#### One reminder with observable delivery
+
+- **Description**: A confirmed action receives one reminder through a configurable provider with visible delivery status.
+- **Acceptance Criteria**: Provider selection is configuration; status is pending, sent, or failed; duplicate delivery is prevented.
+- **Priority**: P0.
+
+### Platform Requirements
+
+- **Web**: Browser UI for facilitator review.
+- **Mobile/Desktop**: No native application in pilot scope.
+
+### Integration Requirements
+
+| System | Type | Purpose | Priority |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Reminder provider | Configurable outbound adapter | One reminder and delivery status | P0 |
+
+## Non-Functional Requirements
+
+### Reliability
+
+- A reminder is neither scheduled nor sent before confirmation and is attempted no more than once.
+- Delivery status remains observable after the provider attempt.
+
+### Security
+
+- Workspace isolation applies to transcripts and actions and is proven with the real request role.
+- No regulated health or payment data is in scope.
+
+### Usability
+
+- The facilitator LARP must reach upload, review, confirmation, and delivery status.
+- Confirmation visibly communicates the approval point.
+
+## Design Principles
+
+### User Experience Principles
+
+1. Proposals are never presented as sent actions.
+2. Review precedes consequence: text, owner, and due date require deliberate confirmation.
+3. Status answers the next question: pending, sent, or failed is visible.
+
+### Technical Principles
+
+1. Use the supplied Python API and browser UI stack.
+2. Keep delivery behind a configurable provider adapter.
+3. Enforce workspace isolation in the request path, not the UI.
+4. Build slices that can prove the required end-to-end path and negative probe.
+
+## Constraints & Assumptions
+
+### Business Constraints
+
+- Two engineers and a four-week, five-team pilot.
+- Recording meetings, calendar replacement, and autonomous outbound messaging are non-goals.
+
+### Technical Constraints
+
+- Input is uploaded transcripts; Pulseboard does not record meetings.
+- Reminder provider is not selected.
+
+### Assumptions
+
+1. Pilot teams can supply transcripts; otherwise revisit input, not the recording non-goal.
+2. An owner has a provider-addressable identity; otherwise delivery remains visibly blocked.
+3. The provider can return a delivery result; otherwise status must report attempt, not receipt.
+
+## Release Strategy
+
+### MVP Scope
+
+Secure transcript upload and extraction, human review and confirmation, then one observable reminder attempt.
+
+### Release Phases
+
+**Phase 0**: E000 establishes tests, CI, configuration, deployment, and observation.
+
+**Phase 1**: E001 establishes workspace-private extraction, review, and confirmation.
+
+**Phase 2**: E002 delivers one reminder attempt and visible status.
+
+### Rollout Strategy
+
+- Onboard five pilot teams.
+- Gate live provider delivery per workspace until operation is proven.
+- Disable delivery attempts to roll back while retaining honest proposal, confirmation, and status records.
+
+## Success Metrics
+
+| Type | Metric | Target | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Business | Pilot teams | 5 | Pilot roster |
+| User | Proposal review rate | >=80% | Workspace-scoped events |
+| User | Unowned overdue actions | 30% reduction | Baseline and weekly review |
+| Technical | Cross-workspace read success | 0 | Real request-role negative probe |
+| Technical | Reminder behavior | <=1 attempt per confirmed action | Delivery records |
+
+## Risk Analysis
+
+| Risk | Probability | Impact | Mitigation | Owner |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Provider remains unselected | M | H | Configurable adapter; do not claim live delivery without a selected provider | Engineering |
+| Ambiguous extraction | M | M | Editable proposals and required confirmation | Product/engineering |
+| Workspace exposure | L | H | Request-path isolation and negative probe | Engineering |
+| Low review rate | M | H | Weekly metric review and facilitator LARP | Pilot facilitator |
+
+## Dependencies
+
+- External: provider selection, credentials, and delivery-status capability before live delivery.
+- Internal: E000 enables E001; E001's confirmed action enables E002.
+
+## Appendices
+
+- **Research data**: none; this plan uses only workspace evidence.
+- **Architecture**: Python API, browser UI, workspace-scoped data, configurable provider adapter; detailed architecture is deferred because no foundation artifact was supplied.
+- **Glossary**: proposal = unapproved extracted action; confirmed action = facilitator-approved text, owner, and due date; workspace = tenant boundary.
+
+## Revision History
+
+| Version | Date | Changes | Author |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1.0.0 | 2026-08-10 | Initial plan from supplied foundation | Project planning |
